<BlogPost slug="iptv-vs-cable">
  <p>Both IPTV and cable let you watch live television, but they deliver it in very different ways, and each has real trade-offs. This guide compares them point by point so you can decide what suits your household.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th><span class="sr-only">Feature</span></th><th>IPTV (e.g. Prime IPTV)</th><th>Traditional cable TV</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>How it arrives</strong></td><td>Over your internet connection</td><td>Over the provider's cable network</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Equipment</strong></td><td>A compatible device and a player app you already own or can install</td><td>Provider-supplied set-top box</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Channels</strong></td><td>Very large lineups (Prime IPTV: 25,000+ live channels)</td><td>Packages with a fixed number of channels per tier</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>On-demand library</strong></td><td>Large VOD library (Prime IPTV: 120,000+ movies &amp; series)</td><td>Usually a smaller on-demand selection</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Screens</strong></td><td>TVs, phones, tablets and computers, within your connection limit</td><td>Usually tied to boxes in specific rooms</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Contract</strong></td><td>Prime IPTV has no contract and offers cancellation</td><td>Often term-based agreements</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Depends on home internet</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Quality</strong></td><td>SD, HD, FHD and 4K where supported</td><td>Depends on package and box</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Channels and content</h2>
  <p>Cable bundles channels into packages, so a bigger lineup usually means a higher tier. IPTV services typically offer a far larger catalogue in one subscription, plus a large on-demand library. The practical question is not how many channels exist but whether the ones you watch are included, which is exactly what a trial is for. Our <a href="/blog/iptv-free-trial-checklist/">24-hour trial checklist</a> shows how to test that.</p>

  <h2>Equipment and devices</h2>
  <p>Cable relies on equipment from the provider. IPTV runs on devices you may already have: Smart TVs, Fire TV and Android TV, Apple TV, phones, tablets and computers. See the <a href="/setup/">supported device guides</a>.</p>

  <h2>Cost and flexibility</h2>
  <p>Cable pricing varies by provider, package and region, and often includes equipment fees and agreements for a set term. Prime IPTV publishes its prices openly: plans for 1 to 4 connections from <a href="/pricing/">$34.99 CAD for 3 months</a>, with 6- and 12-month plans getting +1 month free and 24-month plans +2 months free. There is no contract, and a 7-day money-back guarantee applies.</p>

  <h2>What you give up with IPTV</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dependence on your internet.</strong> If your home connection is slow or unstable, streams will suffer. Wired connections help; see <a href="/blog/iptv-buffering-troubleshooting/">buffering and login fixes</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>A little setup.</strong> You add a login to a player once. It takes minutes with our <a href="/setup/">step-by-step guides</a>, but it is not a plug-in box.</li>
    <li><strong>The established cable infrastructure.</strong> Cable is a mature, regulated service with a long track record. IPTV providers vary widely, so it is worth testing before you commit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you gain</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A much larger channel and on-demand library in one subscription.</li>
    <li>Use on several screens, with plans for up to 4 connections.</li>
    <li>No installation visit and no contract.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
  <p>If you want maximum simplicity and have no reliable broadband, cable may still suit you. If you have a stable internet connection and want a bigger lineup on more screens without a contract, IPTV is worth testing. The lowest-risk way to find out is to <a href="/free-trial/">try Prime IPTV free for 24 hours</a> (no credit card required) and compare it with what you watch today.</p>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Can I keep cable and also use IPTV?</h3>
  <p>Yes. IPTV runs over your internet connection, so it can run alongside an existing cable package while you compare.</p>
  <h3>Do I need fast internet for IPTV?</h3>
  <p>You need a stable connection, roughly 10 Mbps for HD and 25 Mbps for 4K per screen. See the speed requirements guide for the full table.</p>
  <h3>Is there a contract with Prime IPTV?</h3>
  <p>No. There is no contract, and cancellation is available.</p>
  <h3>How can I compare them fairly?</h3>
  <p>Test your must-have channels, a live event at peak time and the on-demand library during the 24-hour free trial.</p>
</BlogPost>
